The alternative method is to assign a name to the previously bound `Pet` `py::class_` object and reference it when binding the `Dog` class, passing it as the parent class object:

```cpp
py::class_<Pet> pet(m, "Pet");
pet.def(py::init<const std::string &>())
    .def_readwrite("name", &Pet::name);

// Method 2: pass parent class_ object:
py::class_<Dog>(m, "Dog", pet /* <- specify Python parent type */)
    .def(py::init<const std::string &>())
    .def("bark", &Dog::bark);
```

This approach specifies the Python parent type (via the `pet` object) rather than the C++ base class as a template parameter.

On the event {Dₙ = d}, monicity gives, for 1 ≤ i ≤ r:

[t^{-(n+1)}]Qₙαᵢ = u⁽ⁱ⁾_{n+d} + Σ_{k=0}^{d-2} θ_{n,k} u⁽ⁱ⁾_{n+1+k}

where the sum is FTn-measurable.
